don't clobber memory volumes with aisand prune

DONE (2026-08-08): pre-allowing tool permissions is moot — aisand now
passes --dangerously-skip-permissions unconditionally, since the
container is the trust boundary. No settings.json allow-list needed.

investigate whether claude update can be made to work for the
non-root container user (e.g., installing claude into $HOME instead
of system-wide). Currently users must aisand rebuild.

consider whether to mount more of the host's claude config than just
the API key approval list — e.g., model preferences, custom commands.

handle host with no ~/.gitconfig: warn the user that git commit will
fail until they configure one (on the host or inside the container).

can the claude code prompt itself be modified to show when it is in the sandbox?

aisand.json extra_packages is apt-only, so there is no first-class path
for pip-only or newer-than-Debian Python deps (bookworm gives numpy
1.24.2, matplotlib 3.6.3, scipy 1.10.1 — no numpy 2.x). Two dead ends
worth remembering: system pip is blocked by PEP 668
(/usr/lib/python3.11/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED), and pip install --user does
not survive a run because $HOME is tmpfs. Only a venv inside the repo
persists, since the repo is bind-mounted rw — but that puts build
artifacts in the source tree and costs a rebuild on every fresh
container.
Idea: a pip_packages key that builds a venv into the derived image
(e.g. /opt/venv, created at image build time, on PATH via ENV), so the
install cost is paid once per package-set change like extra_packages
already is. Do NOT reach for --break-system-packages.
Open question: whether pip_packages and extra_packages should share
one hash label or get separate ones.
